Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples of Jesus came to him and asked him, "Who is the greatest person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He placed the child among them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
He who becomes free from pride like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever receives a little child like this in my name, receives me.
But what if someone leads one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to be thrown into the sea with a large millstone tied around his neck.
How dreadful it is for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like this must come. But how dreadful it is for those who cause them!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. And in hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Make sure you don't underestimate any of these little ones. That's what I'm telling you. Their angels in heaven can go at any time to visit their Father who is in heaven.
No
What do you see? Imagine a man who owns 100 sheep, and one of them goes missing. Wouldn't he leave the 99 sheep in the mountains? Wouldn't he go out to search for the one that was lost?
What I am going to tell you now is true. If that sheep exists, it will be happier with it than with the ninety-nine that have not perished.
Thus is your father who is in heaven; he does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When one of them wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep that between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have won back his heart.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take one or two more with you. The scripture says, "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter will be established." (Exodus 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you is true. What you shut up on earth will be shut up in heaven. What you open on earth will be opened in heaven.
Again, here is what I say. Imagine that two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, I am there with them." "A merciless servant"
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times should I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Is seven times enough?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
Kingdom of Heaven as a king who wanted to collect all the money that his servants owed him.
When the king started doing this, a man with millions of dollars in debt was brought to him.
The man was unable to pay, so his master ordered it. The man, his wife, his children, and all his possessions had to be sold to pay his debt.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
The lady felt sorry for him, forgave him his debt, and let him go.
But then that waiter came out and found one of the other waiters who had some dollars on him. He grabbed him and started choking him. "Pay me what you owe me!" he said.
The other slave fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened, and it greatly disturbed them. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then he summoned the first servant, saying, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that debt because you begged me.
Why did you not show mercy to the other slave as you showed mercy to me?
In his anger, his master handed him over to the guards. He will be punished until he pays all that he owes.
Thus will the heavenly Father do unto every one of you, if you do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. What's wrong with them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read that at the beginning the Creator made them male and female (Genesis 1:27)?"
The man said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. A man shall not tear apart what God has joined.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command that a man should give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart, but it was not like that from the beginning.
Here is what I say: Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman is an adulterer. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus replied, saying, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom God has helped to live without marrying can accept that.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And some were made that way by others. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever can accept living that way must do so." The little children are brought to Jesus
Some people brought to Jesus little children, and they wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus placed his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the rich young man.
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good works must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one good God. If you want to enter the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony."
Honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And "you shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commands." "What should I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. Then you will have treasure in heaven. And then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I tell you is true. It is hard for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. And they asked, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With people, it is impossible, but with God, all things are possible."
Peter answered and said, "We have left everything behind to follow you! What reward will be given to us?"
"What I am about to tell you is true," Jesus said to them. "When all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then, you who have followed me will sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel."
Every person who has left their home, their family, or their field because of me will receive a hundredfold more than that, and they will also receive eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them their usual wage for a day of work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
Around nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace square doing nothing.
He told them, "Go also and work in my vineyards. I will pay you what is right."
They went off. "He went out again around noon and at three, and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock he went out and saw others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long and do nothing?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. "He said to them, 'Go also you and work in my vineyard.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Begin with the others who were hired recently, and then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived, and each one received their usual daily wage.
When the first of those hired arrived, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received their usual daily wage.
When they received that, they began to doubt its owner.
They said, "These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour." "You paid them the same amount that you paid us. We did most of the work and stayed under the scorching sun all day."
The owner replied to one of them saying, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary that you gave them.
Do I not have the right to do whatever I want with my own money? Do you feel deceived because I gave others so generously?
Those who are last now will be first, and those who are first now will be last.' Jesus speaks again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem." "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to the Gentiles. They will mock him, spit on him, flog him, and kill him. And after three days, he will rise from the dead!" Or do you ask Jesus for a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus, and her sons came with her. She knelt down before him and asked him a favor.
What do you want? Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my two sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise that the other will sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking." "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I will drink?" "We can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places are for those for whom they are prepared by my Father."
The other ten pupils heard about it and were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called out to them, saying, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; for they exercise authority over them, and those who are great exercise authority over them.
Do not be like that. Instead, whoever among you wants to be great must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first must be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give His life as a ransom for many people. (Two blind men received their sight)
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They cried out, saying, "Lord, son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd told them to be quiet. They were asked to keep silent. But the two men shouted all the louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood up and called out to them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Sir," they replied, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus' entry into Jerusalem.
As they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethany, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He told them, "Go to the village that is before you. As soon as you arrive, you will find a camel that is tied up. It will have its little camel with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, say that the Lord needs them. The Owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. It says,
Say to the daughter of Zion, "Behold, your king is coming to you; he is just and having salvation, lowly and riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
Bring the donkey and the day horse. They threw their cloak on them. Then Jesus sat on the cloaks.
A very large crowd gathered and spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed. They all cried out, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the entire city was stirred up. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus drove them out of the temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the money changers. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord said: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer.' (Isaiah 56:7) And you have made it a den of robbers." (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and lame came to Jesus at the temple, and he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple courts shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they became indignant.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you never read it in the Scriptures: 'I have made you, even as the children and the sucklings praise You.' " (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus went away from the people and walked out of the city to Bethany. And He spent the night there. And the fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He came up to it but found nothing on it but leaves. And he said to it, "May you never bear fruit!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. And they asked, "How did the tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus answered and said to them, "What I tell you in darkness, speak in light. What you hear in the ear, preach on the rooftops. And do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather, fear Him who can destroy both soul and body in hell. Are not two sparrows sold for a copper coin? And not one of them will fall to the ground apart from your Father. But the very hairs of your head are all numbered. Do not fear, therefore; you are of more value than many sparrows. And He spoke a parable to them: 'Can the blind guide the blind? Will they not both fall into a pit?' The disciple is not above his teacher, but everyone when he is fully trained will be like his trainer. Why do you see the speck that is in your brother's eye, but do not notice the log that is in your own eye? Or how can you say to your brother, 'Let me remove the speck from your eye,' while there is a log in your own eye? Hypocrite! First remove the log from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother's eye. For there is no good tree that produces bad fruit, nor is a bad tree that produces good fruit. For every tree is known by its own fruit. For men do not gather grapes from thorn bushes, nor do they gather figs from thistles; for grapes are not gathered from thorn bushes, nor are figs gathered from thistles. Every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit. A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit. Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire. Therefore, by their fruits you will know them. 'Do not judge so that you will not be judged. For with the judgment you pronounce you will be judged, and with the measure you use it will be measured to you. Why do you see the speck that is in your brother's eye, but do not notice the log that is in your own eye? Or how can you say to your brother, 'Let me remove the speck from your eye,' while there is a log in your own eye? You hypocrite! First remove the log from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother's eye. For there is no good tree that produces bad fruit, nor is a bad tree that produces good fruit. For every tree is known by its own fruit. For men do not gather grapes from thorn bushes, nor do they gather figs from thistles; for grapes are not gathered from thorn bushes, nor are figs gathered from thistles. Every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit. A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit. Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire. Therefore, by their fruits you will know
"If you have faith, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." "His authority is questioned"
Jesus entered the temple court. While he was teaching there, the chief priests and the scribes came to him. And they asked him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, saying, "I will also ask you one question. If you answer me, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From whence came John's baptism? From heaven, or from men? They disputed among themselves about this. They said, "If we say it is from heaven, he will ask, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, saying, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Therefore, I will not tell you either by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons."
What do you see in this? A man had two sons, and he went to the first one and said to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
'I will not,' the son answered. But afterwards he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing. The son answered, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
"Who of the two sons did the will of his father?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God before you."
John came to teach you the right way to live. And you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed in him. You saw that. But even at that time you did not turn away from your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Here's another story. There was a man who owned some land, and he planted vineyards. He put up a wall around them. He dug a trench for a grape press inside. He also built a watchtower. He ran the vineyards for some tenant farmers. Then he left on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He commanded the servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
In the end, he sent his son to them. He said, "They will fear my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will receive all the owner's possessions someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard comes back, what will he do to those tenants?
"The evil people will be destroyed," they answered. "Then the vineyard will be rented to other farmers. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was spoken to you by God: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is from the Lord, it is wonderful in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
Thus I tell you, the kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to those who produce its fruit.
Anyone who falls on that stone will be crushed into pieces. But the stone will press on anyone who falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They realized he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowds. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is like: A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those who had been invited to the dinner. The servants told those who accepted to come, but they refused.
Then he sent some extra servants. "Tell the invited guests," he said, "that I have prepared the wedding supper. I have killed my oxen and my fattened cattle. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding supper."
But people did not pay attention. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
Here's the translation: I kept the change as a service. They treated them badly and killed them in the end.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed the murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But the guests were not worthy of the occasion."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, good and bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not dressed in the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without the wedding clothes?" The man couldn't think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him out into the darkness. There people will groan and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and schemed to entangle Jesus in his own words.
They sent their followers to him. And they sent with them some Herodians. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a good man. You teach the way of God truthfully. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. You do not care how important they are."
So then tell us, what do you think? Is it right that we pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intentions. He said, "Oh, you who claim to be prophets! Why are you trying to ensnare me?
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." "Bring him a silver coin."
He asked them: "Whose picture is this? And whose are the words?"
"Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's. And give to God what is God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
They said, "Teacher," here is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry his widow. He must have children by her to carry on his brother's name.
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no sons, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. This continued until it reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? She was married to all seven.
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures. And you do not know the power of God.
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the resurrection of the dead? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living.
When the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching. The most important commandment
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees were unable to answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was a scholar of the law, and he tested Jesus with a question.
"My teacher," he asked, "what is the most important commandment in the Law?"
Jesus replied, saying, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind." (Matthew 22:37-38)
This is the first and most important thing.
And the second is like it. "Love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets is based on these two commandments. Who is the son of Christ?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?" "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David calls him "sir," then how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any other question.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
"The scribes and Pharisees sit in the seat of Moses," he said.
So you must obey them. Do everything they tell you. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They bind heavy luggage and place it on the shoulders of other people. But they are not willing to lift a single finger to move it.
Everything they do is done so that others may see it. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing verses from the Koran. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the ribbons on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also like to get the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the city markets. They like to be called "the rabbi."
But no one should call you "rabbi". You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth "father." You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
No one should call you "Master." You have one master, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be a servant to you.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.
How much more dreadful will it be for you, you teachers of religious law and you Pharisees! You are the ones who are being hypcritical! You are the ones who are shutting the Kingdom of Heaven in people's faces. You yourselves will not enter, nor will you allow those who are entering to do so.
No
Woe to you who teach the law and are Pharisees! You cross sea and land to make a single convert, and then you make them twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
How can you be blind guides? You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing.' But whoever swears by the gold of the temple, he is bound by his oath.'
You are blind and foolish! What is the most important thing? Is it gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say, "If anyone takes an oath by the name of the altar, it is nothing. But if anyone takes an oath by the name of the gift on it, he must fulfill his vow."
Uncle! What is more important? Is it the giving? Or is it the altar that makes the giving sacred?
For anyone who swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears by the temple, swears by its name and by whoever dwells within it.
And whoever swears by the sky, swears by the throne of God and by whoever sits upon it.
How terrible you are, you scientists in the law and Pharisees! You pretenders! You give to God the tenth of your spices, such as mint and dill and cumin. But you have not practiced the more important matters of the law, such as justice and mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting the former.
O guides of the blind! You remove the smallest insect from your food. But you swallow a whole camel!
Woe to you who teach the law and the Pharisees! You hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside they are full of greed and self-indulgence. You blind guides!
Blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the dish. Then the outside will also be clean.
Oh, what a terrible thing this is for you, you scientists in the law and you Pharisees! You who are full of hypocrisy! You are like tombs that are painted white on the outside. They look beautiful and clean from the outside. But on the inside, they are full of the bones of the dead. They are also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
This applies to you as well. From the outside, it looks like you are doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are filled with what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Woe to you who teach the law and you Pharisees! You who boast and claim! You build the tombs of the prophets and you decorate the graves of the righteous.
And she says: "If we had lived in the days of those who preceded us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You bear witness against yourselves, acknowledging that you are the descendants of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the sins that those who lived before you started!
O den of vipers! How will you escape being condemned to hell?
I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, crucify some of them, and flog others in your synagogues and pursue them from town to town.
You will pay with the blood of all righteous humans whose blood has been shed on earth. I mean from the blood of the righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is real. All of this will happen to those who are alive now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. I have repeatedly wanted to gather your people, to be like a hen gathering her chicks under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say: "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him in secret. They said to him, "Tell us. When will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Be careful! Watch out for yourselves, so that your hearts may not be burdened and overloaded with anxiety."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Messiah!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not worry. These things must happen. But the end has not come yet.
Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines, and pestilences in many places.
All of this is the beginning of labor pains.
Then people will hand you over to be abused and killed. All nations will come to hate you because of me.
In that time, many will depart from their faith. They will exchange hatred. They will betray one another.
Many false prophets will appear. They will mislead many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever perseveres until the end will be saved.
This good news about the kingdom will be preached in the whole world for a witness to all nations; then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the abomination that makes desolate" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11). One day you will see him, the one who is destined to destroy the city and the sanctuary. The reader must understand this.
Then those who are in Judea should flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
How dreadful it would be in those days for pregnant women! How terrifying it would be for nursing mothers!
They prayed that they would not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than at any time since the beginning of the world until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time wasn't shortened, no one would survive. But thanks to the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "He is over there!" Do not believe it.
False christs and lying priests will appear. They will do great signs and wonders. They will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone says, "He is deep inside the house," do not believe him.
Lightning, seen approaching from the east, will be visible in the west. Likewise, when the Son of Man comes, it will be the same.
Vultures will gather wherever a carcass is found.
"Immediately after the terrible sufferings of those days, 'the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken.' (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)"
Then, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven. He will come with power and great glory.
His angels will be sent with a loud trumpet sound. He will gather His chosen people from all four directions. They will be brought from the ends of the heavens to their other ends.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches become soft and put forth leaves, you know that summer is near.
Similarly, when you see all those events happening, you know the end is near. It's right at the door.
What I am going to tell you is true. The people living at that time will certainly not die until all those things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or that hour, not even the angels in heaven. The Son does not know, only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea whatsoever what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Two women will be ground up using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other left.
Therefore beware, you do not know on what day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Imagine if the homeowner knew exactly what time of night the thief would come. He would guard the house. He would not allow his home to be broken into.
Therefore you also must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Imagine a master putting one of his servants in charge of the other servants in his house. The servant's job is to distribute food to them at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant to do this.
It will be good for the servant if his master finds him doing his work when the master returns.
What I am about to tell you is real. This master will give this slave authority over all that he owns.
But what if the slave is wicked? What if he says to himself, "My master is absent for a long time"?
What if he starts hitting the other servants? And what if he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily?
The master of that slave will return on a day the slave does not expect. And he will return in an hour the slave does not know.
Then the master will cut it into pieces. He will send it to the place where the competitors go. There they will express their grief and grind their teeth in anger.
The Story of Ten Dolls
Thus shall be the kingdoms of heaven at that time. And ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were ignorant. Five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they didn't take any olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in bottles with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. All the bride's friends got tired and slept.
At midnight someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the groom's female relatives woke up and prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
They went to buy oil. But while they were going, the groom arrived. The brides, who were ready with the groom, entered the wedding dinner. Then she closed the door.
Later, the rest of the bride's friends came too. They said, "Sir! Sir! Open the door for us!"
But he answered and said, "What I tell you is true. I do not know you."
Beware. You do not know the day or the hour when the Bridegroom will come. The Parable of the Three Workers.
Again, here is how the Kingdom of Heaven will be. A man traveling sent his servants and entrusted them with his possessions.
The man gave 10 thousand dollars to one servant. He gave 4 thousand dollars to another servant. He gave 2 thousand dollars to the third servant. The man gave each servant an amount of money that he knew the servant was capable of taking care of. Then he set off on his journey.
The server who received 10 thousand dollars immediately went and put his money to work. He earned another 10 thousand dollars.
The one who won 4000 dollars won an additional 4000 dollars.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received 10 thousand dollars brought the other 10 thousand. He said: "Sir, you placed your trust in me with 10 thousand dollars. Look, I have earned another 10 thousand dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share in your master's joy!"
The man who had $4000 also came. He said, "Sir, I have deposited $4000 in your trust. Look, I have earned another $4000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share in your master's joy!"
Then came the man who received $2000. He said, "Sir, I knew you were a difficult man. You reap where you did not sow. You gather crops where you did not plant the seeds."
I was afraid. I went out and buried your $2000 in the ground. Look, here is your money.
His master answered him, "You lazy enemy! Don't you know that I gather where I did not sow? Don't you know that I reap where I did not sow?
Well, then you should have deposited my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have withdrawn it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants saying: "Take 2000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20000 dollars."
To those who have much, even more will be given, and they will have more than enough. But for those who have nothing, what is it? Even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Remove this valueless wretch from here. There in the darkness, people will mourn and crush their teeth. "Sheep and camels."
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory. And with him will come all the angels. Then he will sit on his glorious throne. And the people of all nations will be gathered before him.
All nations will assemble before him. He will divide the people into two groups. He will be like a shepherd separating sheep from goats.
He will put the sheep to his right and the goats to his left.
Then the king speaks to those on his right, saying, "Blessed is my father. Come, inherit the realm prepared for you from the creation of the world."
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous will answer, saying, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you something to drink?'
When did we ever see you as a stranger and invite you in? And when did we ever see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and went to visit you?
The king will answer, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did for me."
Then he says to those on his left, "Cursed ones! Depart from me into the everlasting fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels."
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not let me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick in prison. But you did not care for me.
They will also say, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or in need of clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will answer, "What I am telling you is the truth. If you do nothing to anyone less important than themselves, you will not do it to me."
Then they go to their punishment forever. But whoever does good will receive eternal life.
The plot to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples:
You know that Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They met in the palace of the high priest, Caiaphas.
They planned to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
But not during the feast," they said. "Or the people may become stirred up." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethlehem. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a specially sealed flask of very expensive perfume. She poured the perfume on his head as he sat at the table.
When the students saw this, they were angry. "Why is this being wasted?" they asked.
The perfume could be sold for a high price. The money could be given to the poor.
Jesus perceived this. He said to them, "Why do you bother this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
There will always be poor people with you. But you will not always have me.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What you have done will be told everywhere this good news is published throughout the world. It will be told in her memory."
One of the twelve went to the priests of the summit. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked them, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they said to him, "Thirty pieces of silver."
Since then, Judas watched for an opportune time to deliver Jesus to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. They asked, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover meal?"
He answered and said, "Go into the city to a certain man. Tell him, 'The Teacher says, My time is at hand. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was at the table with the twelve.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The pupils became very sad. They began to tell him, one after the other, "It wasn't me, sir, was it?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me will betray me.
The Son of Man will go just as it is written about him. But woe to the one through whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would be better for him if he had not been born.
Joseph was the one from whom he would be delivered." He said, "It is not I, my Lord; am I not aware?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is you."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. He thanked, broke it and gave it to his disciples saying: "Take it and eat it. This is my body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked them, handed it over to them. And he said: "Drink from it all of you."
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is poured out to forgive the sins of many.
Here is what I say to you. From now on, I will never drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "In this very night, all of you will desert me." He wrote, "The Lord said, 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.'
Peter replied, "Everyone may walk away from you, but I never will."
"What I tell you is true," Jesus answered. "This very night before the rooster crows, you yourself will disown me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. And he said to them, "Sit here, while I go yonder and pray."
Peter and my son Zechariah went with him. He began to feel sad and worried.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. "Couldn't you men keep watch with me for one hour?" Peter asked.
Be alert and pray. This way you will not fall into sin when tempted. The spirit is willing. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out a second time. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not what I will, but what You will."
Then he came back. Once again he found them sleeping. They could not keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. And for the third time he prayed with the same supplication.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look! The hour is near. The Son of Man will be delivered over to the sinners."
"Arise! Let us go! Here is he whom they delivered up to them!" Jesus is arrested
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas came. He was one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him, carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests and the elders of the people had sent them.
Joseph, who was going to betray Jesus, had made a sign with them. He said: "The man whom I shall kiss is the same". "Arrest him".
Judas immediately went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Teacher." And he kissed him.
Jesus replied, "My friend, do what you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples reached for his sword. He drew it and struck the high priest's servant. He cut off the servant's ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place. For whoever uses the sword will perish by the sword.
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But then how are the sacred texts fulfilled? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowds, saying, "Am I not guiding an armed group against you? Should you come with swords and sticks to arrest me? I used to sit every day in the courtyard of the pyramid, teaching, and you did not arrest me."
But all this happened so that the words of the prophets would be fulfilled." All his disciples left him and fled. Jesus was taken to the Sanhedrin
Those who arrested Jesus brought him to the high priest Caiaphas. He gathered there the scholars of the law and the elders.
It wasn't too far, Peter followed Jesus. He came right up to the courtyard of the high priest. He went in and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and the entire Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they didn't find any evidence, even though many false witnesses appeared. In the end, two more witnesses appeared.
They said, "This man has been claiming, 'I can destroy God's temple. I can rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the High Priest stood up and questioned Jesus, saying, "Do you not hear the testimony that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus did not answer. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Yes. It is as you say, Jesus answered. "But now I say to all of you. In the days to come, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Power. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the High Priest tore his clothes, and said, "He has spoken great blasphemy against God! Why do we need any more witnesses? You have heard him say these blasphemous things."
What do you think? "He must die!" they answered.
Then they threw it in his face. They assaulted him with their hands. Others hit him.
They said, "Tell us, O Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter said, "I do not know Jesus."
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him. She said to him, "You were also with Jesus the Galilean."
But in front of them all, Peter said he wasn't. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the door of the courtyard. There another woman saw him and said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said it again, "He isn't." Swearing, "I don't know the man!"
After a short time, the people who had been standing there approached Peter. "You must be one of them," they said. "The way you talk gives you away."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore, saying, "I do not know this man!" And immediately a cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "A rooster will not crow," Jesus had told him. "Before that happens, you will have denied me three times." Peter went out. He broke down and wept bitterly.
Judah hangs himself.
That was early in the morning. All the chief priests and elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away, and they delivered him to Pilate the governor.
Joseph, whom he handed over, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. And he reckoned on what he had done, with deep shame and sorrow. So he gave back to the chief priests and the temple guards the thirty pieces of silver.
"I have sinned," he said. "I handed over an innocent man." "What's it to us?" they replied. "That's your problem."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and went out. After this, he departed and hanged himself.
The high priests took the money. "It is forbidden," they said, "to put this money in the temple treasury. It is blood money. It paid for a man's death."
So they decided to use the money to buy a cemetery field, where people from other countries would be buried.
That is why it is still called the Field of Blood to this day.
Then the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of my blood, which the people of Israel had valued."
Use the money to buy the Potters' Field, as the Lord has commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) "Jesus is brought before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and the elders accused him, he made no answer.
Then Pilate asked Jesus, saying, "Do you not hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word, not even to a single charge. The provincial governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the Governor's custom at the Easter picnic to pardon one convict. The people got to choose who they wanted.
At that time, they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you: Barabbas, or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. "Have nothing to do with that man," she said, "for he is innocent. I have suffered greatly over this dream I had today because of this man."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus executed.
"Which do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
Then what shall I do with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. They all answered, "Crucify him!"
Why? And what crime has he committed? I asked Pilate. But they screamed even louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere. Instead, the crowd began to grow angry. So he took water and washed his hands in front of them. "I am not responsible for the death of this man," he said. "You are the one responsible for it!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas, but ordered Jesus to be flogged. Then he handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They removed his clothes and put a brown cloak on him.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Then they fell down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. "We honor you, King of the Jews!" they said.
They cursed him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After they had mocked him, they stripped him of the robe. They put his own clothes back on him. Then they led him away to crucify him. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Sirte called Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and offered it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
When they seized him and hung him on the cross, they divided his garments among them by casting lots.
They sat down and followed him there.
They placed over his head the title, which read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
He was stuck with two robbers. One on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads.
And he said, "So you are going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself then! Get down from the cross if you are the son of God!"
Similarly, the priests, the leading scholars, and the elders mocked him.
"Save others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He wishes to put his trust in God. God is indeed aware of whom He will save now if He wills it. He is the one who said, "I am the Son of God."
Similarly, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus mocked him. Jesus died.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those standing there heard Jesus cry out and said, "He is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, someone ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. He offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see whether Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus loudly called out, he died.
At that moment, the temple curtain was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook. The rocks split.
The graves opened. The corpses of many righteous people who had died were revived.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus was raised from the dead, they entered the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and the guards who were with Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened; and they were filled with awe and said, "Truly he was the Son of God!"
It was not very far, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to meet his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. There was also Mary, the mother of James and Joseph. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was there also. Jesus was buried.
As evening approached, a wealthy man from the city of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered it to be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of cloth.
He placed it in his new grave which was hewn out of the rock. And he pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day was the day after the preparation day. The leading priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
Master, they said, "We remember something that liar said while he was still alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
So he ordered that the tomb be made secure until the third day. If you do not, his disciples may come and take away the body. Then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has come back to life. This last lie will be even worse than the first."
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the grave secure, placed a seal on the stone, and posted guards for the watch.
Jesus rises from the dead.
It was now Saturday evening. The dawn had come on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled back the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, as he said! Come, see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from death. He will go before you to Galilee. There you will see him." And now I have told you.
The women ran from the tomb. They were afraid but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly Jesus met them. "Peace be upon you," he said. So they came near him, and they took hold of his feet and expressed their respect to him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." And the guards reported these words to the high priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They offered the soldiers a large sum of money.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will give him a bribe. This will keep you out of trouble.
The soldiers took the money and did as they were commanded. This story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last commands to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they prostrated to him. But some still had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you can be sure that I will be with you to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, "I will send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger cries in the desert: "Prepare the way for the Lord, make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from the region of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him. When they acknowledged their sins, he baptized them in the Jordan River.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach: "After me will come one who is more powerful than I am. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals.
"I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." (The Baptism and Temptation of Jesus)
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that exact moment, he saw the sky split apart. He saw the Holy Spirit come down on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven spoke to him, saying, "You are my son, whom I love; I am very pleased with you."
In a moment, the Holy Spirit sent Jesus into the desert.
He was in the desert for forty days. There the devil assassinated him. Wild animals did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose his first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He preached the good news of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking along the shores of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their nets into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further. As he did this, he saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People admired his teaching style. He taught them as if he were an authority. He did not speak like legal scholars.
At that very moment, a man cried out in their temple. He was under the control of an evil spirit. He said:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
Jesus firmly said, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The devils violently raged the man. Then she came out of him with a cry.
The people were all astonished. And they said to each other, "What is this? It is a new commandment! And with great authority! And he even commands the evil spirits, and they obey him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those with him left the temple. They went straight, along with James and John, to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her body. Then she began to serve them.
After sunset on that evening, the people brought to Jesus all the sick and all those who were possessed by demons.
All the people of the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them, and they suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also drove out many evil spirits. But He did not allow them to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was early in the morning and the darkness was still present. Jesus arose and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus replied, "Let us go to another place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there also. That is why I came."
He traveled throughout Galilee. He preached in their churches. He also cast out demons. Jesus heals a man with a skin disease
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and said to Jesus, "If you are willing to make me clean, it is within your power."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do it," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He was cured.
He immediately dispatched his Jesus. And he gave the man a stern warning.
"Don't tell anyone about that," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make sacrifices as Moses commanded. That will be proof to the priest and to the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began talking at once. He spread the news to all the people. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city openly. He stayed outside in deserted places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus returned to Capernaum. The people heard that he had returned to his own town.
So many people gathered that there was no more room. There wasn't even room outside the door. And Jesus taught them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get him close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made an opening in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his stretcher through the opening.
Jesus saw their faith, and said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some law professors sitting there. They were thinking.
Why is this person speaking this way? He is saying something very evil! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately, Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"; or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He told me, "Get up. Take your rug and go home."
The man stood up, took his prayer rug, and walked away, while everyone was watching. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus was dining at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some law professors who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that, so he said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to follow those who believe they are right with God, but I came to follow sinners." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees would fast without eating. Some people came to Jesus. They said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; and in that day they will go out into the field, and will not find it.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto an old garment. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old. This would make the hole even worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wineskins. If they did, the wine would burst the wineskins and the wine would be ruined along with the wineskins. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. "Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath."
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the grain fields. The disciples began to pull out some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, it is against the law to do this on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them: "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.
"For the Son of Man is even Lord of the Sabbath."
Healing on Saturday
At another time, Jesus entered the temple. There was a man there who had a withered and crooked hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with the weak and deformed hand. "Stand up in front of everyone," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say about what to do on the Sabbath? Is it to do good or to do evil? Is it to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger. He was very angry because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as if it was new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd from Galilee followed them.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the region of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from gathering around him.
Jesus healed many people. So, the sick were pushed towards him to touch him.
When the people saw those possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. From then on, they were with him, and he also sent them out to preach.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve, and Simon was among them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Yoda Judas Iscariot was also one of them. He was the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Baal Zebul
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. They went to take his affairs upon themselves. And they said, "He has lost his mind."
There were some law professors. They came from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebul! He casts out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus called them to him and spoke to them through stories. He said, "How can Satan cast out Satan?
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
